
  [1mThe fork:[0m whether I'm designing notifications [3mfor an app that exists somewhere I haven't seen[0m, or designing the slice
  of a task app that makes notifications meaningful in the first place. These lead to completely different specs — the
  first is an integration design constrained by your existing model and stack, the second is a greenfield design where I
  pick the task model and the notification system together.

  - [1mExisting app elsewhere[0m — point me at it and the design is shaped by what's already there: your data model, auth,
    stack. Cheap to get right, expensive to guess wrong, since a spec written against an imagined model is throwaway.
  - [1mGreenfield, this repo is the start[0m — I design tasks + notifications together. Full freedom, but I'd be picking your
    stack and persistence story, and those are the expensive-to-change decisions.
  - [1mSingle-user/local only[0m — "users" is really just you, notifications are in-page or browser-level reminders.
    Dramatically smaller: no server, no accounts, no delivery infrastructure. Rules out multi-user later without a
    rewrite.

  My recommendation: if this repo genuinely is the app, the honest answer is that "notifications" is the second
  subsystem, not the first — there's nothing to notify about yet. I'd scope this conversation to the greenfield option
  and decompose, designing the task model with a change-event seam built in, then notifications on top of it as its own
  spec.
